WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.900
Genome sequencing allows us to actually
identify and pinpoint mutations 

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.100
which can then help us to understand what brought about the cancer in the first place.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.300
We've sequenced several Tasmanian Devil
Tumour Disease genomes now

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:19.100
and we identified a mutation involved in
one gene in the Tasmanian devil cancer

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.200
known as platelet-derived growth factor A or PDGFA for short. 

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:31.000
A number of human cancers have mutations in the PDGFA gene

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.200
or in the PDGFA receptor.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.400
There are a number of drugs that have been developed

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.000
which specifically inhibit the PDGFA
receptor 

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:44.100
and these have been found to be quite effective in some kinds of human cancers.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.900
So this leads to some interesting possibilities

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.200
of using this drug in the Tasmanian devil cancer

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.530
so could perhaps be an effective treatment in this cancer too.

